 ## Overview
-Hierarchical Vector Quantization on Delta is a compression method for time-varying volumetric data that combines temporal delta encoding with hierarchical vector quantization (HVQ) [S1]. It solves the problem of efficiently storing and rendering large dynamic volume datasets by exploiting temporal coherence between adjacent frames. Unlike static HVQ which encodes spatial redundancy, this variant first computes the difference (delta) between consecutive time steps, then applies HVQ to these residuals, resulting in smaller quantization ranges and higher compression ratios for smooth temporal transitions [S1].
+Hierarchical Vector Quantization on Delta is a compression method for time-varying volumetric data that combines temporal delta encoding with hierarchical vector quantization (HVQ) to achieve high compression ratios while maintaining direct rendering capabilities on programmable graphics hardware. It sets itself apart from sibling methods like Temporal/Spatial Vector Quantization by explicitly decoupling temporal redundancy (via delta encoding) from spatial redundancy (via HVQ), allowing the quantization codebooks to adapt to the residual data distribution rather than the raw data [S1].
 
 ## How it works
+The method operates on time-varying volume data represented as a sequence of 3D grids. The core innovation is the application of delta encoding across time steps before applying the spatial compression.
+
 **Inputs and Outputs:**
-- Input: A sequence of volumetric data frames $V_t$ for $t = 0, \dots, T-1$.
-- Output: A compressed bitstream representing the delta-encoded residuals quantized via HVQ.
